# Lesson 4, batch 2: nothing to lose in diamonds. West leads a low diamond; dummy's K5 goes up and wins, and nine tricks follow without giving up the lead.
title: The king goes up
id: L04-31
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.3
student: S
board: 115
dealer: S
vulnerable: EW

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  9752   K82    K5     KQT9
  E  QJ3    QT54   QT7    643
  S  AK4    A63    863    AJ52
  W  T86    J97    AJ942  87

auction:
  S 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 16 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 8
  spades: 2 = The ace and king.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king.
  diamonds: 0 = Dummy's king wins only if West, who led the suit, has the ace.
  clubs: 4 = Eight clubs with the ace, king, queen and jack.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: West led a low diamond. Who might have the ♦A?
play:
  1   W D4  N DK*  E D7  S D3
      DK key: Nothing to lose. If West has the ace, the king wins now. If East has it, the king was never going to win a trick.
      DK not D5: Did you think about playing the king? If you play low, East plays a high diamond, and later the ace captures your king. If West has the ace, the king wins right now.
  2   N CK  E C3  S C2  W C7
      note: The king won: that's your ninth trick. Now take your nine without giving up the lead. You're in dummy, so start with the clubs.
      ok: H S
  3   N CQ  E C4  S C5  W C8
  4   N CT  E C6  S CJ  W S6
      note: Overtake with your jack, to reach your ♣A.
  5   S CA  W S8  N C9  E DT
      note: Four club tricks.
  6   S SA  W ST  N S2  E S3
      note: Now the spades and hearts.
      ok: H
  7   S SK  W H7  N S5  E SJ
  8   S H3  W H9  N HK  E H4
  9   N H2  E H5  S HA  W HJ
      note: That's nine tricks.
  10  S H6  W D2  N H8  E HT
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  11  E HQ  S S4  W D9  N S7
  12  E SQ  S D6  W DJ  N S9
  13  E DQ  S D8  W DA  N D5
      note: 9 tricks: 3NT made.

play recap:
  The plan: eight obvious winners, and dummy's ♦K as the ninth if West, who
  led the suit, had the ace.

  Playing the king cost nothing. If East had held the ace, the king would
  have been lost anyway. West had it, so the king won, and you took nine
  tricks without ever giving up the lead. Give West the lead, and the
  defense would have taken four diamonds.
